; ======================================================================
;  screen_source.pi4 - THE DISPLAY SEAM.
;
;  A screen this monitor can put its console on is a framebuffer plus a
;  way to present it. This file is the whole of what the two kinds of
;  screen on a Raspberry Pi 4 have in common, and the whole of what makes
;  them different.
;
;  ---- WHY THERE IS A SEAM AT ALL -------------------------------------
;
;  Until today "the screen" meant one thing: ask the VideoCore firmware
;  for a framebuffer, draw on what it hands back. That is HDMI, and it
;  works because the firmware brings HDMI up before this program loads.
;
;  It does not work for the DSI panel and it never can. The firmware
;  never brings DSI1 up on this board - the block's power domain is off
;  at boot and nothing in config.txt on the bench stick tells it
;  otherwise - so the firmware owns NO DSI display. `screen displays`
;  asks the firmware and gets zero, and that answer is correct: there is
;  no firmware display. The panel is lit by this monitor, out of this
;  monitor's own memory, through a display list this monitor writes.
;
;  So the two screens differ in exactly one thing - WHERE THE
;  FRAMEBUFFER COMES FROM - and are identical in everything after it.
;  That difference is what this file is. Above it, screen_cmd.pi4 builds
;  the same console on whichever surface it is given. Below it,
;  display.pi4 draws on whatever surface it was installed with
;  (DisplayInit for the firmware's, DisplayAdopt for ours).
;
;  THE SEAM IS NOT A FLAG. There is no `If dsi` inside a drawing
;  primitive, inside the console grid, inside the cursor, or inside the
;  keyboard. Those four never learn which screen they are on.
;
;  ---- WHAT A SOURCE HAS TO ANSWER -------------------------------------
;
;    ScrSourceName(src)     what to call it in a sentence
;    ScrSourceCount(src)    how many of this kind the board has now
;    ScrSourceUp(src, idx)  acquire a surface and install it
;    ScrPresent(y0, y1)     make rows y0..y1 of what was drawn visible
;
;  HDMI's present is empty because the firmware scans the same bytes the
;  console draws into. The DSI panel's present is empty too at rotation
;  0 and 180, and is a transpose at 90 and 270 - see THE ROTATION below.
;
;  ---- INCLUDE ORDER ---------------------------------------------------
;
;  After display.pi4 (the library and the board's), hvs.pi4,
;  dsi_host.pi4, dsi_panel_v2.pi4, dsi_panel_v2_dcs.pi4, i2c.pi4,
;  mmu.pi4, memmap.pi4 and Anvil/Core/settings.pbi. Before
;  screen_cmd.pi4, which drives it.
; ======================================================================


; ======================================================================
;  THE ROTATION, AND WHAT IT COSTS - READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING IT
;
;  THE PANEL IS PORTRAIT AND THAT IS NOT A SETTING. The Waveshare 10.1
;  scans 800 pixels across and 1280 down; the pixel valve was programmed
;  with those numbers and read them back (PV_HORZB 00CF0320,
;  PV_VERTB 00140500). Nothing this program does changes the direction
;  the panel's own driver ICs walk their rows.
;
;  THE HVS HAS NO 90 DEGREE ROTATION. Not "it is not wired up here" -
;  the block does not have one. vc4_plane.c:2782-2786 declares the
;  complete set of rotations a VC4/VC5 plane can be asked for:
;
;      DRM_MODE_ROTATE_0 | DRM_MODE_ROTATE_180 |
;      DRM_MODE_REFLECT_X | DRM_MODE_REFLECT_Y
;
;  and vc4_plane.c:1979-1985 reduces whatever DRM asked for down to those
;  before a single display-list word is written. There is no ROTATE_90
;  bit to set. 180 degrees is the two mirrors at once, and both mirrors
;  are free - one bit each in POS0, no pixel moved.
;
;  SO 90 AND 270 ARE THE RENDERER DRAWING TRANSPOSED, AND THAT IS THE
;  WHOLE COST:
;
;    * TWO BUFFERS INSTEAD OF ONE. The console draws into a landscape
;      1280x800 buffer (#MON_FB_DRAW) and the panel scans the portrait
;      800x1280 one (#MON_FB_SCAN). At 0 and 180 they are the same
;      buffer and the second is untouched memory.
;
;    * A TRANSPOSE ON EVERY REPAINT. One logical row is one COLUMN of
;      the scanned picture, so it is not a memcpy in either direction.
;      It is done damage-driven - only the rows the console actually
;      repainted - and walked so that the DESTINATION runs are
;      contiguous, which is the half that matters: the loop below steps
;      the scanned buffer's rows on the outside and writes a short
;      contiguous run of each. A one-line change to the log is a few
;      tens of thousands of pixels; a full-screen scroll is all
;      1,024,000 of them.
;
;    * DMA SCROLL STAYS IN THE LOGICAL DRAW BUFFER. A turned row is not
;      contiguous in the scanned buffer, but it is contiguous in
;      #MON_FB_DRAW, so ScreenFastScroll still moves it there. The present
;      then transposes the whole changed console band. V3D does better: it
;      renders the rebuilt grid already transformed into a physical back
;      buffer, then DMA presents one contiguous frame to #MON_FB_SCAN.
;
;  0 AND 180 KEEP EVERY FAST PATH, AND THE DEFAULT IS NOT ONE OF THEM.
;  The ruling of 2026-09-08 is that the panel is used landscape, because
;  that is how it fits on the bench. It is bolted to the frame long edge
;  across, so a console that scans the way the panel scans is a console
;  standing on its side, and the fastest possible way to draw an
;  unreadable screen is not a saving. The default is 90 and the cost
;  above is paid deliberately, every repaint. `screen rotate 0` gets the
;  fast paths back for anyone who wants the 100 x 72 portrait console;
;  it is one command and it is saved.
;
;  WHAT THE COST ACTUALLY IS, IN NUMBERS, SO IT IS NOT A VAGUE WORRY.
;  The panel is 1,024,000 pixels. A full repaint transposes all of them;
;  a one-line change at 16 x 32 cells transposes 32 logical rows, which
;  is 32 x 1280 = 40,960 pixels, about a twenty-fifth of the screen. The
;  console is a monitor's log, not a game: it repaints when somebody
;  types or a line is printed.
; ======================================================================

; THE CONSTANTS AND THE ARITHMETIC ARE IN screen_geom.pi4, NOT HERE.
; #SCR_SRC_*, the >= 80 column font rule, the rotation map, the boot
; choice and the transpose are all functions of their arguments, and
; they live in a file with no hardware in it so that
; tools/a64/a64_dsiscreen_check.py can EXECUTE them - the real image,
; on the emulator, on a machine with no VideoCore. Everything below
; this line touches registers, I2C, the mailbox or the settings store,
; which is exactly why it is not there.

Global gScrSrc.i    = #SCR_SRC_NONE  ; what the running screen is on
Global gScrRot.i    = 0              ; the rotation IN EFFECT, degrees
Global gScrScale.i  = 1              ; the font magnification IN EFFECT.
                                     ; Assigned in ONE place, ScreenUpOn's
                                     ; shared tail, and read by every report.
Global gScrDsiUp.i  = 0              ; 1 once the DSI pipeline is scanning
Global gScrDsiSeen.i = -1            ; -1 not looked yet, 0 no panel
                                     ; answered, 1 the panel micro did
Global gScrDsiStep.i = 0             ; the bring-up step that stopped, or 0
Global gScrDsiLine0.i = 0            ; the scan line counter, read twice
Global gScrDsiLine1.i = 0            ; 20 ms apart. Different = scanning.

; The scratch the rotation is turned into text in before it is handed to
; SettingsSet, which takes a NUL-terminated string and not a number.
; Four bytes of digits at the outside; sixteen so nobody has to think
; about it again.
Global Dim gScrRotBuf.a[16]

; The same, for `screen scale`. A separate buffer rather than a shared
; one: the two commands never run at the same time today, and a shared
; scratch is the kind of saving that is correct until the day somebody
; sets both from one line.
Global Dim gScrScaleBuf.a[16]

; The bring-up steps, so a failure names itself rather than printing a
; number. See ScrDsiBringUp.
#SCR_DSI_STEP_DOMAIN = 1
#SCR_DSI_STEP_I2C    = 2
#SCR_DSI_STEP_MCU    = 3
#SCR_DSI_STEP_PLAN   = 4
#SCR_DSI_STEP_CLOCKS = 5
#SCR_DSI_STEP_HOST   = 6
#SCR_DSI_STEP_PANEL  = 7
#SCR_DSI_STEP_HVS    = 8


Procedure ScrSourceName(src.i)
  If src = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    Print("the DSI panel")
  ElseIf src = #SCR_SRC_HDMI
    Print("HDMI")
  Else
    Print("no display")
  EndIf
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE DSI PANEL AS A SCREEN
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ScrDsiPanelSeen - IS A PANEL PLUGGED IN? One I2C conversation, and it
;  costs nothing when the answer is no.
;
;  The panel's micro is at $45 on the DISPLAY connector's bus, and it
;  answers three read-only identity registers. Reading them proves more
;  than an address ACK does: three bytes came back from a part that had
;  to be addressed, hold a register number across a repeated start, and
;  drive data. It does NOT prove any differential lane works - the I2C
;  pair and the DSI lanes are different wires in the same flat cable -
;  but a ribbon that carries I2C is seated, and a board with nothing
;  plugged in cannot fake it.
;
;  IT DOES NOT POWER ANYTHING. DsiV2Identify() reads; the rails are
;  DsiV2Begin()'s job and that happens in the bring-up, only if this
;  said yes. A board with no panel therefore does no DSI work at all at
;  boot - no domain message, no clock change, no PHY - which is the
;  property that makes this safe to run on every boot of every board.
;
;  The bus is selected and PUT BACK, the same contract touch_cmd and
;  dsi_cmd use, so a later `i2c scan` cannot report this bus's devices
;  under a heading naming the header pins.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ScrDsiPanelSeen()
  Define ok.i

  If gScrDsiSeen >= 0
    ProcedureReturn gScrDsiSeen
  EndIf

  gScrDsiSeen = 0
  I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_DISPLAY)
  If I2cUp() <> 0
    If DsiV2Identify() <> 0
      If DsiV2Plausible() = 1
        gScrDsiSeen = 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf
  I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_HEADER)
  ProcedureReturn gScrDsiSeen
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ScrDsiBringUp - THE PANEL, FROM DARK TO SCANNING.
;
;  This is the seventh pass's sequence made a library path. It is NOT
;  the `dsi` probe commands: those print a page each, are written to be
;  read by somebody at a prompt, and stop where a probe should stop.
;  This runs at boot, prints four lines, and either works or says which
;  step did not.
;
;  THE ORDER IS THE DRIVER'S AND IT IS NOT NEGOTIABLE:
;
;   1 the firmware power domain          vc4_dsi.c:937 - pm_runtime first
;   2 the panel's rails, over I2C        panel-waveshare-dsi-v2.c
;   3 the link clock and the D-PHY       vc4_dsi.c
;   4 the panel's DCS init table         200 commands, needs 3 to carry it
;   5 the HVS channel over the buffer    vc4_hvs.c
;   6 the pixel valve, then video, then
;     VIDEN last                         vc4_crtc.c:657-703
;
;  NOTHING HERE CAN STRAND THE BOOT. Every step is bounded by its own
;  library's timeouts, the panel's init table gives up after a run of
;  failures rather than grinding through 200 of them, and a failure at
;  any step returns 0 with the serial line and the network console
;  untouched. The one call that could take time on a board with no panel
;  - all of it - is never reached, because ScrDsiPanelSeen() said no.
;
;  It is idempotent: called twice, the second call re-runs the sequence
;  on hardware that is already up, which is what `screen on dsi` typed
;  at a running panel has to do anyway.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ScrDsiBringUp()
  Define ok.i

  gScrDsiUp = 0
  gScrDsiStep = 0

  ; ---- 1. the power domain, before any DSI1 register is touched ------
  ; Without this every register below reads back perfectly and the block
  ; drives nothing at all. It is the whole finding of 2026-09-08.
  If DsiDomainOn() = 0
    gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_DOMAIN
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ---- 2. the panel's rails ------------------------------------------
  ; The rails come BEFORE the host: a host brought up against lanes
  ; nobody is holding latches a contention error before anything has
  ; been asked of it, and an unpowered panel holds nothing.
  I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_DISPLAY)
  If I2cUp() = 0
    I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_HEADER)
    gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_I2C
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DsiV2Begin() = 0
    I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_HEADER)
    gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_MCU
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ok = DsiPanelRails()
  If DsiV2Backlight(255) = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_HEADER)
  If ok = 0
    gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_MCU
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ---- 3. the link clock and the D-PHY -------------------------------
  If DsiModePlan(70000000) = 0
    gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_PLAN
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DsiClocksSet() = 0
    gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_CLOCKS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  DsiHostBringUp()
  If DsiIsUp() = 0
    gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_HOST
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ---- 4. the panel's own init table, over the link just built -------
  ; DsiV2Begin() AGAIN, and it is not redundant: the reference sequence
  ; that produced a lit panel on this board ran the expander's power-on
  ; write before the rails and again before the table, and this is that
  ; sequence rather than a tidied version of it. Rule 21 - reproduce
  ; what was measured; a rearrangement is a new experiment.
  ;
  ; The backlight goes back on AFTER it, and that has to be said out
  ; loud: DsiV2Begin() rewrites the whole sixteen-bit expander shadow to
  ; the probe state, which CLEARS the backlight enable. A perfect picture
  ; behind a dark backlight is indistinguishable from no picture at all,
  ; and it is exactly the self-inflicted blank screen that gets blamed on
  ; the flat cable.
  I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_DISPLAY)
  ok = 1
  If DsiV2Begin() = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  If ok <> 0
    If DsiPanelPrepare() = 0
      ok = 0
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If DsiV2Backlight(255) = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_HEADER)
  If ok = 0 Or DsiPanelSent() = 0
    gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_PANEL
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ---- 5 and 6. the HVS over our buffer, then the valve, then video --
  ; The buffer is cleared first. The HVS starts scanning the instant the
  ; channel is enabled, and whatever DRAM held at #MON_FB_SCAN would
  ; otherwise be the first thing on the glass.
  HvsFill(#MON_FB_SCAN, #MON_FB_PANEL_W, #MON_FB_PANEL_H, $FF000000)
  MmuCleanRange(#MON_FB_SCAN, #MON_FB_BYTES)

  ; The mirrors before the list is written, so the list carries them.
  If gScrRot = 180
    HvsMirror(1, 1)
  Else
    HvsMirror(0, 0)
  EndIf

  HvsUp(#MON_FB_SCAN, #MON_FB_PANEL_W, #MON_FB_PANEL_H)

  ; The vertical numbers are the panel's own timing, unchanged from the
  ; sequence that scanned on this board: 1280 active, sync 1300..1320,
  ; total 1324.
  PvUp(#MON_FB_PANEL_W, DsiHsyncS(), DsiHsyncE(), DsiHtotal(), #MON_FB_PANEL_H, 1300, 1320, 1324)
  PvEnable()
  DsiVideoOn()
  PvVideoOn()

  ; ---- IS IT ACTUALLY SCANNING? THE LINE COUNTER, AND NOTHING ELSE ---
  ; Every register above this line can be perfect over a channel that is
  ; standing still. Two reads of the line counter with a different
  ; number in them is the one measurement that says pixels are moving,
  ; and it can only be taken HERE - after the valve is running - which
  ; is why it is not HvsUp's to make. See THE READBACK in hvs.pi4.
  gScrDsiLine0 = HvsChannelLine()
  delay(20)
  gScrDsiLine1 = HvsChannelLine()
  If gScrDsiLine0 = gScrDsiLine1
    gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_HVS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  gScrDsiUp = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ScrDsiSayStep - name the step that stopped, as a sentence with the
;  first thing to check in it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure ScrDsiSayStep()
  If gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_DOMAIN
    PrintN("!! the firmware would not power the DSI block (domain 19). Nothing")
    PrintN("   in it works until that domain is on, and the firmware is the only")
    PrintN("   thing that can turn it on. Check that the firmware on the boot")
    PrintN("   medium answers the power-domain tags: dsi domain prints every")
    PrintN("   word of that conversation.")
  ElseIf gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_I2C
    PrintN("!! the display connector's I2C bus would not come up, so the panel's")
    PrintN("   rails could not be switched on. Nothing was changed. This is the")
    PrintN("   controller, not the panel: try i2c bus display then i2c scan.")
  ElseIf gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_MCU
    PrintN("!! the panel's micro at address 45 answered its identity registers")
    PrintN("   and then would not take a power-on write, so the panel may be")
    PrintN("   part powered - which is worse than not powered. Reseat the flat")
    PrintN("   cable at both ends and reset the board.")
  ElseIf gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_PLAN
    PrintN("!! the display PLL could not be read, so no divider could be chosen")
    PrintN("   and no clock was programmed. Nothing was changed.")
  ElseIf gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_CLOCKS
    PrintN("!! a clock generator would not go idle inside its bound, so the DSI")
    PrintN("   clocks are in an unknown state and the PHY was not brought up.")
    PrintN("   Reset the board; dsi clocks shows what CPRMAN holds.")
  ElseIf gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_HOST
    PrintN("!! the DSI host did not come up, so there is no link for the panel's")
    PrintN("   init table to travel on. dsi host prints every register it")
    PrintN("   programmed and what each one read back.")
  ElseIf gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_PANEL
    PrintN("!! the panel did not take its init table. The link is built and the")
    PrintN("   rails are on, so the suspect is the four differential lanes in")
    PrintN("   the flat cable - the I2C pair is proven and they are not. dsi")
    PrintN("   power is the read only a live panel can answer.")
  ElseIf gScrDsiStep = #SCR_DSI_STEP_HVS
    PrintN("!! the panel is lit and the whole pipeline is programmed, but it is")
    PrintN("   NOT SCANNING: the scaler's line counter read the same number")
    Print("   twice 20 ms apart (")
    PrintDec(gScrDsiLine0)
    Print(" then ")
    PrintDec(gScrDsiLine1)
    PrintN("). Every register can be perfect")
    PrintN("   over a channel that is standing still, and this is the only")
    PrintN("   reading that tells the two apart. dsi pipe prints the whole")
    PrintN("   channel and the pixel valve.")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ScrDsiSurface - install the console's drawing surface for the current
;  rotation. The panel always scans #MON_FB_SCAN; the console draws
;  there too unless it is sideways, in which case it draws into
;  #MON_FB_DRAW and ScrPresent moves it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ScrDsiSurface()
  Define lw.i
  Define lh.i
  Define fb.i

  lw = ScrLogicalW(gScrRot, #MON_FB_PANEL_W, #MON_FB_PANEL_H)
  lh = ScrLogicalH(gScrRot, #MON_FB_PANEL_W, #MON_FB_PANEL_H)

  fb = #MON_FB_SCAN
  If ScrSideways(gScrRot) <> 0
    fb = #MON_FB_DRAW
    ; The drawing buffer is cleared here rather than left to the console's
    ; own first clear: until the first present it is never scanned, so
    ; nobody would see stale DRAM - but a transpose of uninitialised
    ; memory into the corner of the panel that the console has not
    ; painted yet would be visible, and this is one pass over 4 MB at
    ; boot.
    HvsFill(fb, lw, lh, $FF000000)
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn DisplayAdopt(fb, lw * 4, lw, lh, 32)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  ScrPresent - MAKE WHAT WAS DRAWN VISIBLE.
;
;  y0..y1 are LOGICAL rows, inclusive, in the console's own coordinates.
;  The renderer calls this with the band it just painted.
;
;  At rotation 0 and 180 on either screen, and on HDMI at any rotation
;  it is offered, this does nothing at all and costs one comparison: the
;  bytes the console drew into are the bytes being scanned. The 180 case
;  is turned round by the scaler's mirror bits, after this.
;
;  At 90 and 270 it is the transpose, and THE LOOP ORDER IS THE POINT.
;  A logical row is a column of the scanned picture, so one of the two
;  sides has to be walked with a stride whatever happens. The scanned
;  buffer's rows are on the OUTSIDE and a short contiguous run of each
;  is written on the inside, because a strided READ costs a cache line
;  that is mostly wasted while a strided WRITE costs a read-modify-write
;  of a line that is entirely wasted - and because the cache maintenance
;  the scaler needs is then one clean of a contiguous run rather than
;  one per pixel.
; ======================================================================
Procedure ScrPresent(y0.i, y1.i)
  Define sy.i
  Define lh.i
  Define run.i

  If gScrSrc <> #SCR_SRC_DSI
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If ScrSideways(gScrRot) = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gScrDsiUp = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  lh = ScrLogicalH(gScrRot, #MON_FB_PANEL_W, #MON_FB_PANEL_H)
  If y0 < 0
    y0 = 0
  EndIf
  If y1 > lh - 1
    y1 = lh - 1
  EndIf
  If y1 < y0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; The pixels, then the maintenance. Both walk the scanned buffer's
  ; rows and both get the run's address from ScrRunAddr, so a clean can
  ; never cover bytes the transpose did not write.
  ScrTranspose(gScrRot, #MON_FB_DRAW, #MON_FB_SCAN, #MON_FB_PANEL_W, #MON_FB_PANEL_H, y0, y1)

  run = ScrRunBytes(y0, y1)
  sy = 0
  While sy < #MON_FB_PANEL_H
    ; Clean to the point of COHERENCY, not unification: these bytes are
    ; about to be read by another master. With the caches off this is a
    ; loop that changes nothing, which is the right price for not having
    ; to remember whether they are on.
    MmuCleanRange(ScrRunAddr(gScrRot, #MON_FB_SCAN, #MON_FB_PANEL_W, lh, y0, y1, sy), run)
    sy = sy + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ScrPresentAll - the whole console, for a full repaint and for the
; banner band, which is not part of the console grid.
Procedure ScrPresentAll()
  ScrPresent(0, ScrLogicalH(gScrRot, #MON_FB_PANEL_W, #MON_FB_PANEL_H) - 1)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE GPU RENDER TARGET AND PRESENT CONTRACT
;
;  V3D renders in the same LOGICAL coordinates as the console. These
;  accessors describe the PHYSICAL raster target beneath that canvas.
;  HDMI keeps its firmware surface. DSI renders into Anvil's existing
;  #MON_FB_DRAW back buffer and presents the finished physical frame to
;  #MON_FB_SCAN, which the HVS owns. No firmware framebuffer call belongs
;  on the DSI path.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i ScrGpuBase()
  If gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_DRAW
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn DisplayBase()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ScrGpuPitch()
  If gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_PANEL_W * 4
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn DisplayPitch()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ScrGpuPhysicalW()
  If gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_PANEL_W
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn DisplayWidth()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ScrGpuPhysicalH()
  If gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_PANEL_H
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn DisplayHeight()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ScrGpuRotation()
  If gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_DSI And ScrSideways(gScrRot) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn gScrRot
  EndIf
  ; DSI 180 is already done by the HVS mirrors. HDMI exposes the orientation
  ; of its firmware surface and needs no renderer transform here either.
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ScrGpuBytes()
  ProcedureReturn ScrGpuPitch() * ScrGpuPhysicalH()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ScrGpuHasFixedBack()
  If gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; Snapshot the surface that is ACTUALLY PRESENTED, in one call. Callers keep
; these eight values for their whole operation; they never temporarily alter
; DisplayBase/geometry and never infer the front from the renderer's current
; target. DSI always presents the physical portrait HVS front. HDMI presents
; the firmware view (offset zero today, but DisplayViewBase is authoritative).
;
; w/h are logical capture dimensions; panelW/panelH/pitch describe the physical
; raster beneath them. ScrCapturePixel applies the one common rotation map.
Procedure.i ScrCaptureSnapshot(*base, *w, *h, *pitch, *rot, *panelW, *panelH, *bpp)
  Define fb.i
  Define pw.i
  Define ph.i
  Define p.i
  Define d.i
  Define r.i

  If gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    If gScrDsiUp = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    fb = #MON_FB_SCAN
    pw = #MON_FB_PANEL_W
    ph = #MON_FB_PANEL_H
    p = pw * 4
    d = 4
    r = gScrRot
  ElseIf gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_HDMI
    fb = DisplayViewBase()
    pw = DisplayWidth()
    ph = DisplayHeight()
    p = DisplayPitch()
    d = DisplayBytesPerPixel()
    r = 0
  Else
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If fb = 0 Or pw < 1 Or ph < 1 Or p < (pw * d) Or d <> 4
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PokeI(*base, fb)
  PokeI(*w, ScrLogicalW(r, pw, ph))
  PokeI(*h, ScrLogicalH(r, pw, ph))
  PokeI(*pitch, p)
  PokeI(*rot, r)
  PokeI(*panelW, pw)
  PokeI(*panelH, ph)
  PokeI(*bpp, d)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Write one LOGICAL overlay pixel straight to the surface being presented.
; This is intentionally distinct from ScrPresent: a sideways DSI V3D back
; buffer is already in physical layout, so treating its untouched rows as a
; logical drawing surface would transpose unrelated pixels into the front.
; Small service overlays use this seam; normal renderers keep their existing
; damage/present paths. The cache clean is per pixel because callers are tiny
; (the alive glint is 5 pixels plus two 5x5 restores), never a raster renderer.
Procedure.i ScrPresentedPixel(x.i, y.i, colour.i)
  Define fb.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define pw.i
  Define ph.i
  Define lw.i
  Define lh.i
  Define rot.i
  Define px.i
  Define py.i
  Define at.i

  If gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    If gScrDsiUp = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    fb = #MON_FB_SCAN
    pw = #MON_FB_PANEL_W
    ph = #MON_FB_PANEL_H
    pitch = pw * 4
    rot = gScrRot
  ElseIf gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_HDMI
    fb = DisplayViewBase()
    pw = DisplayWidth()
    ph = DisplayHeight()
    pitch = DisplayPitch()
    rot = 0
  Else
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  lw = ScrLogicalW(rot, pw, ph)
  lh = ScrLogicalH(rot, pw, ph)
  If fb = 0 Or x < 0 Or y < 0 Or x >= lw Or y >= lh
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  px = ScrMapX(rot, x, y, pw, ph)
  py = ScrMapY(rot, x, y, pw, ph)
  at = fb + py * pitch + px * 4
  PokeL(at, colour)
  MmuCleanRange(at, 4)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Begin the DSI copy at a frame boundary. There is no interrupt-driven WSI
; yet, so this bounded poll watches the HVS line counter wrap. A dead channel
; costs at most 20 ms and the copy still proceeds; presentation must never
; turn a stopped line counter into a stopped console.
Procedure ScrDsiWaitFrameStart()
  Define before.i
  Define now.i
  Define t0.i

  If gScrDsiUp = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  before = HvsChannelLine()
  t0 = Micros()
  Repeat
    now = HvsChannelLine()
    If now < before
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    before = now
  Until (Micros() - t0) >= 20000
EndProcedure

; Preserve the already-correct banner from the DSI front buffer after V3D
; clears and redraws the back. At 90/270 the logical top band is a physical
; vertical strip; at 0/180 it is the ordinary top strip. This copies pixels
; already transformed by the established CPU path, so the photo and smooth
; fonts do not acquire a second renderer or a second rotation formula.
Procedure.i ScrGpuKeepBanner(renderBase.i)
  Define x.i
  Define y.i
  Define w.i
  Define h.i
  Define row.i
  Define src.i
  Define dst.i
  Define pitch.i

  If gScrSrc <> #SCR_SRC_DSI
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If renderBase <> #MON_FB_DRAW
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  pitch = #MON_FB_PANEL_W * 4
  x = 0
  y = 0
  w = #MON_FB_PANEL_W
  h = LogoHeight()
  If gScrRot = 90
    x = #MON_FB_PANEL_W - LogoHeight()
    y = 0
    w = LogoHeight()
    h = #MON_FB_PANEL_H
  ElseIf gScrRot = 270
    x = 0
    y = 0
    w = LogoHeight()
    h = #MON_FB_PANEL_H
  EndIf

  src = #MON_FB_SCAN + y * pitch + x * 4
  dst = #MON_FB_DRAW + y * pitch + x * 4
  If gDma <> 0
    If DmaCopy2D(dst, pitch, src, pitch, w * 4, h) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  EndIf

  row = 0
  While row < h
    DspCopyUp(dst + row * pitch, src + row * pitch, w * 4)
    row = row + 1
  Wend
  ; The GPU is finished before this routine, so only the later present reads
  ; these CPU writes. Make them complete before that DMA transfer starts.
  a64_barrier()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Present one complete GPU frame. DSI has a fixed physical back buffer and
; no mailbox page flip: DMA copies it to the HVS front after render complete.
; If the DMA tier is unavailable, the existing unrolled CPU mover is the
; correctness fallback. HDMI's double-height allocation is also presented by
; a copy, preserving the crisp offset-zero behaviour already proven there.
Procedure.i ScrGpuPresent(renderBase.i, bytes.i)
  Define front.i

  If bytes <> ScrGpuBytes()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If gScrSrc = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    If renderBase <> #MON_FB_DRAW Or gScrDsiUp = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    front = #MON_FB_SCAN
    ScrDsiWaitFrameStart()
  Else
    front = DisplayBase()
    If front = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf

  If renderBase = front
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If gDma <> 0
    If DmaCopy(front, renderBase, bytes) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; Honest lowest-tier fallback. DspCopyUp is overlap-safe in this direction
  ; and is also valid for these two disjoint buffers.
  DspCopyUp(front, renderBase, bytes)
  MmuCleanRange(front, bytes)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE ROTATION SETTING
;
;  `screen.rotate` in SETTINGS.TXT, one of 0, 90, 180 or 270. Absent or
;  unreadable means the screen's own default, which on the DSI panel is
;  LANDSCAPE (90) because that is how the panel is mounted on the bench.
;  See WHICH WAY UP THE CONSOLE COMES UP in screen_geom.pi4; the choice
;  itself is ScrRotPick there, so the gate can execute it.
;
;  THIS FILE'S ONLY JOB HERE IS THE STORE. Reading a string out of
;  SETTINGS.TXT and turning it into a number is the part that cannot run
;  on the emulator, so it is the only part that is not in the arithmetic
;  file.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i ScrRotKey()
  ProcedureReturn "screen.rotate"
EndProcedure

; ScrRotWanted - the rotation the named screen will come up at, whatever
; the store does or does not hold.
Procedure.i ScrRotWanted(src.i)
  ProcedureReturn ScrRotPick(src, PmfDecOf(SettingsGet(ScrRotKey()), #SCR_ROT_UNSET))
EndProcedure

; ScrRotStored - what is actually in the store, or #SCR_ROT_UNSET when
; there is nothing usable there. `screen rotate` prints this beside the
; rotation in effect, because "the store is empty and you are seeing the
; default" and "the store says 90" are different sentences and a bench
; deciding whether to type anything needs to tell them apart.
Procedure.i ScrRotStored()
  ProcedureReturn PmfDecOf(SettingsGet(ScrRotKey()), #SCR_ROT_UNSET)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  HOW BIG THE SCREEN IS, AND THEREFORE HOW BIG THE TYPE IS
;
;  THE ARITHMETIC IS IN screen_geom.pi4. This half is the two things
;  that cannot run on the emulator: asking the hardware how big it is,
;  and reading `screen.scale` out of SETTINGS.TXT. The split is exactly
;  the one the rotation already uses - ScrRotPick decides, this file
;  fetches - so the gate executes the deciding.
;
;  THE ORIENTATION NEVER REACHES THE SIZE. Every procedure here answers
;  about THE GLASS: the panel's own 800 x 1280 scan and its diagonal in
;  inches. Only the eighty-column cap is handed a console width, and it
;  can only ever make the type smaller. That is why turning the console
;  cannot change how big a letter is any more.
; ======================================================================

; ScrDiagTenths - the screen's diagonal in TENTHS OF AN INCH.
;
;   DSI   the panel's own microcontroller, register $97, read during
;         ScrDsiPanelSeen() before anything was powered. This board
;         answers $65 = 101 = 10.1 inches.
;   HDMI  nothing can be asked. See THE SIZE OF A SCREEN THAT CANNOT SAY
;         HOW BIG IT IS in screen_geom.pi4: the firmware's
;         GET_PHYSICAL_WIDTH_HEIGHT is in pixels and says nothing about
;         glass, and the EDID that would say is not read yet.
Procedure.i ScrDiagTenths(src.i)
  Define v.i

  If src = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    v = DsiV2Size()
    If ScrDiagUsable(v) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn v
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #SCR_DSI_DIAG_TENTHS
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #SCR_HDMI_DIAG_TENTHS
EndProcedure

; ScrDiagMeasured - 1 when ScrDiagTenths above is a READING off the
; hardware, 0 when it is the stated assumption. Every report that prints
; a density prints this too, because "the panel says it is 10.1 inches"
; and "no screen can say, so a 24 inch monitor is assumed" are different
; sentences and a bench deciding whether to type `screen scale` needs to
; tell them apart.
Procedure.i ScrDiagMeasured(src.i)
  If src = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    If ScrDiagUsable(DsiV2Size()) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ScrPanelPixW / ScrPanelPixH - THE SCREEN'S OWN pixel geometry, which
; is not the console's when the console is turned.
;
; For the panel these are constants, because this monitor sends exactly
; one DSI mode. For HDMI the surface the firmware handed over IS the
; screen - there is no rotation on that path - so the library's own
; numbers are the honest answer.
Procedure.i ScrPanelPixW(src.i)
  If src = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_PANEL_W
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn DisplayWidth()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ScrPanelPixH(src.i)
  If src = #SCR_SRC_DSI
    ProcedureReturn #MON_FB_PANEL_H
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn DisplayHeight()
EndProcedure

; ScrPpi - pixels to the inch on the named screen. The number the boot
; log and `screen` print.
Procedure.i ScrPpi(src.i)
  ProcedureReturn ScrPixelsPerInch(ScrPanelPixW(src), ScrPanelPixH(src), ScrDiagTenths(src))
EndProcedure

; ScrScaleComputed - what the density asks for on this screen, at a
; console this wide. No store, no override: this is the calculation on
; its own, and `screen scale` prints it beside the stored value so the
; two can be compared.
Procedure.i ScrScaleComputed(src.i, logicalW.i)
  ProcedureReturn ScrScaleFor(ScrPanelPixW(src), ScrPanelPixH(src), ScrDiagTenths(src), logicalW)
EndProcedure

; The store.
Procedure.i ScrScaleKey()
  ProcedureReturn "screen.scale"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ScrScaleStored()
  ProcedureReturn PmfDecOf(SettingsGet(ScrScaleKey()), #SCR_SCALE_UNSET)
EndProcedure

; ScrScaleWanted - THE MAGNIFICATION THAT WILL BE USED. One expression,
; and it is the only thing ScreenUpOn calls.
;
; `screen.scale` is NOT a per-screen setting the way `screen.rotate` is.
; A rotation is a fact about how one panel is bolted down and means
; nothing on HDMI; a scale is a statement about the eyes reading it, and
; somebody who wants bigger type wants it on whichever screen the
; console is on. So it applies to both, and it is the calculation - not
; the setting - that makes the two screens differ.
Procedure.i ScrScaleWanted(src.i, logicalW.i)
  ProcedureReturn ScrScalePick(ScrScaleStored(), ScrScaleComputed(src, logicalW))
EndProcedure
